import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const npvCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'cf', label: 'annual cash inflow', min: 100, max: 2000, step: 50, value: 800 },
    { name: 'r', label: 'discount rate', min: 0.02, max: 0.2, step: 0.01, value: 0.08, unit: 'as decimal' },
    { name: 'p', label: 'project cost today', min: 2000, max: 12000, step: 100, value: 6000 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'present value of 5 years of inflows', expr: 'cf*(1 - (1+r)^-5)/r' },
    { label: 'NPV = PV − cost', expr: 'cf*(1 - (1+r)^-5)/r - p' },
    { label: 'rough payback (years)', expr: 'p/cf' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/intermediate/time-value-of-money-npv">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate finance lesson, and it stands on one foundational lesson:
    <em>Compound Interest</em>, which built the compounding machine — money left invested grows
    exponentially. If that machine feels unfamiliar, go back and read the foundational lesson
    first, because this page runs it in reverse, and reversing something you do not understand
    is a good way to get lost. Beyond it you need only percentages and one line of algebra.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain why money arriving later is worth less than money today, and compute a present value;</li>
    <li>use the annuity and perpetuity formulas and say what each symbol does;</li>
    <li>run the NPV decision rule on a project and defend the verdict;</li>
    <li>explain where the discount rate comes from — pure time value plus a risk premium;</li>
    <li>say when IRR and payback mislead, and why NPV is the tiebreaker.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The foundational lesson built the compounding machine: money left invested grows
    exponentially, 1,000 at 8 per cent becomes 1,469 in five years. The time value of money is
    that machine <strong>run in reverse</strong>. If 1,000 today becomes 1,469 in five years,
    then 1,469 arriving in five years is worth exactly 1,000 today — its
    <strong>present value</strong>. Discounting is compounding read backwards, and once you
    can convert any future cash flow into today's money, every financial decision becomes
    comparable: a bond, a factory, a mortgage, a startup. <strong>Net Present Value (NPV)</strong>
    adds up all the present values of a project's future cash flows and subtracts what the
    project costs today. Positive NPV means the project creates wealth above and beyond what
    the same money could earn elsewhere; negative means it destroys wealth slowly enough
    that only bad accounting hides it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the NPV machine — a project that pays for itself or doesn't, depending on the discount rate" widget="sliderLab" config={npvCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the panel is the machine's dashboard. Slide the annual cash
    inflow and the present value of five years of inflows moves in step — double the inflow,
    double the PV. Slide the discount rate the other way: a higher rate shrinks the present
    value, dragging NPV down towards and past zero. Then slide the project cost and find the
    crossover where NPV flips sign — that price is the most the project is worth paying, which
    is exactly the decision NPV exists to make. The rough payback readout ignores the rate
    entirely; compare it with NPV to see how much the time value of money changes the verdict.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> discounting is compounding run in reverse, and NPV adds up today's
    value of every future cash flow minus the cost today. Next: the arithmetic that does the
    converting, and the shortcuts professionals actually use.
  </p>

  <h2>The discounting machine</h2>
  <p>
    Compounding multiplies by (1 + r) each period; discounting divides. The present value of
    an amount F arriving n years from now at discount rate r is
    PV = F ÷ (1 + r)<sup>n</sup>. Work it with real numbers: 10,000 due in 5 years at
    8 per cent is worth 10,000 ÷ 1.08<sup>5</sup> ≈ 6,806 today. Double the wait to 10 years
    and it falls to about 4,632 — distance in time decays value roughly exponentially, which
    is why "a million dollars in forty years" is a modest promise (at 7 per cent: about
    66,600 today) and why lottery jackpots quoted as headline sums are roughly half
    advertised once discounted.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two shortcuts do most of the world's work. A level <strong>annuity</strong> — the same
    cash flow C every year for n years — has PV = C × (1 − (1 + r)<sup>−n</sup>) ÷ r, the
    formula inside the widget. A <strong>perpetuity</strong> (paying forever) collapses to
    PV = C ÷ r: a stream paying 4,000 a year forever is worth 4,000 ÷ 0.04 = 100,000 at a
    4 per cent rate. This one-line formula prices conventional bonds, values dividend
    streams, and tells you that at low interest rates "forever" assets get expensive — the
    deep mechanism behind the 2021 asset-price boom and its 2022 reversal. For quick mental
    arithmetic, the <strong>Rule of 72</strong>: money doubles in roughly 72 ÷ r years —
    at 6 per cent, about 12; at 9 per cent, about 8.
  </p>
  <p>
    Run the annuity formula in the opposite direction and it prices debt. Borrow 200,000 at
    6 per cent for 25 years: the equal annual payment is C = PV × r ÷ (1 − (1 + r)<sup>−n</sup>)
    ≈ 200,000 × 0.06 ÷ 0.767 ≈ 15,600 a year (about 1,300 a month). Multiply by the 25 years
    and you pay roughly 391,000 on a 200,000 loan — nearly double the principal. Nothing was
    hidden; that is simply what time costs at 6 per cent. The same arithmetic in reverse is
    why a pension promise of 40,000 a year for life is a fund of perhaps 700,000–800,000
    today, not 40,000 times the number of years — and why "how much do I need to retire?"
    is a discounting problem before it is a saving problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "paying nearly double the principal means the mortgage is a
    rip-off." It feels like theft because the raw totals sit side by side. But the two sums live
    at different times: the payments arrive year by year over 25 years, and each discounted
    payment is worth less than its face value. Add up the present values of the 15,600 payments
    at 6 per cent and they come to almost exactly the 200,000 borrowed — the lender earned the
    discount rate, nothing more. Comparing un-discounted totals across decades is among the most
    common arithmetic errors in personal finance.
  </p>

  <h2>NPV: the decision rule</h2>
  <p>
    Now assemble a decision. A machine costs 6,000 today and pays 800 a year for 5 years, at
    a discount rate of 8 per cent. The annuity formula gives PV of inflows = 800 ×
    (1 − 1.08<sup>−5</sup>) ÷ 0.08 ≈ 800 × 3.993 ≈ 3,194. NPV = 3,194 − 6,000 ≈
    <strong>−2,806</strong>: reject, emphatically — the simple payback (6,000 ÷ 800 = 7.5
    years) already exceeded the machine's 5-year life, and discounting makes it worse. Cut
    the price to 3,000 and NPV turns positive (+194): buy. The rule is brutally simple —
    <strong>accept if NPV is greater than zero</strong> — and its power is that "greater
    than zero" already accounts for the time value of money, the risk of the cash flows,
    and every alternative use of the capital. NPV is measured in today's money: a project
    with NPV +1,000 has made the owners 1,000 richer in purchasing power right now.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a project that "pays back" is automatically good. Payback
    ignores what the money could have earned elsewhere. Returning your own 6,000 over five
    years returns it <em>devalued</em> — at 8 per cent those repayments are worth only about
    3,200. Every honest investment question is a question about comparison against the
    discount rate, and only NPV (or its cousin IRR) asks it.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the discount rate comes from</h2>
  <p>
    The discount rate is not a technical constant; it is <strong>the whole judgement</strong>.
    It has two components. The first is the pure time value — what riskless money earns (the
    government-bond rate). The second is a <strong>risk premium</strong>: uncertain cash
    flows are discounted harder, because a promised dollar with a 50 per cent chance of
    never arriving must be valued as half a dollar. Formally, forecasters build
    <strong>expected cash flows</strong> — probability-weighted across scenarios: a project
    paying 1,200 in a boom, 800 in the base case, 300 in a bust, weighted 25/60/15, has an
    expected flow of 0.25 × 1,200 + 0.60 × 800 + 0.15 × 300 = 765, and it is 765 — not the
    cheerful 800 — that gets discounted. That move is probability theory earning its keep
    in finance: expected value as the centre of the distribution, and the <em>spread</em> of
    outcomes (its standard deviation) as the risk that sets the premium in the rate.
    Forecasters then apply a rate that compensates
    for how those flows move with the economy. The corporate standard is the
    <strong>weighted average cost of capital (WACC)</strong>: the blended rate the company
    pays to its lenders and shareholders, which is exactly the hurdle a new project must
    beat. Get the rate wrong in either direction and every decision downstream is wrong:
    overstate it and the firm starves itself of good investments (excessively high hurdle
    rates were a documented behaviour of cash-rich firms in the 2010s); understate it and it
    invests in everything, including the projects that quietly destroy value.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the discount rate is the whole judgement — riskless time value plus
    a premium for uncertainty — and expected cash flows are probability-weighted before they are
    discounted. With the inputs settled, the rival decision rules that compete with NPV can now
    be put on trial.
  </p>

  <h2>IRR and the family of rules</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>internal rate of return</strong> is the discount rate that makes NPV exactly
    zero — the project's built-in yield, its "return as a percentage". Managers love it
    ("this project returns 14 per cent") and it usually agrees with NPV. Its famous failure
    modes: when cash flows change sign more than once (a project with a big cleanup cost at
    the end, like a mine or a nuclear plant), there can be <em>two</em> IRRs or none, and
    the number stops meaning anything; and between two mutually exclusive projects of
    different sizes, the small high-IRR project can have lower NPV — and NPV, which counts
    actual wealth created, is always the tiebreaker. The <strong>payback period</strong>
    (years to recover cost) survives in practice as a crude risk screen — fast payback means
    quick escape from uncertainty — but it ignores everything after the payback point and
    all of the time value before it. Real organisations typically screen with payback,
    rank with IRR, and decide with NPV.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the discount rate is just inflation. Inflation is one
    ingredient — a 6 per cent lending rate might decompose into roughly 2 per cent expected
    inflation, 2 per cent real return, and 2 per cent risk and liquidity premium — but
    discounting asks a broader question: what else could this money do, and how sure am I
    of getting paid? A riskless real project in a zero-inflation world still demands a
    positive rate, because capital has opportunity cost. Confusing the two leads to the
    classic error of "discounting at inflation and ignoring risk", which overvalues every
    uncertain project on the desk.
  </p>

  <h2>Investing and financing — the two halves of corporate finance</h2>
  <p>
    Every corporate decision is one of two kinds. <strong>Investing decisions</strong> put
    money into projects — new factories, acquisitions, R&amp;D programmes — and NPV is their
    constitution: invest when the present value of what comes back exceeds what goes in.
    <strong>Financing decisions</strong> raise that money — debt, equity, retained earnings —
    and their cost is precisely the discount rate on the other side of the ledger. The loop
    closes: financing structure affects the discount rate (leverage raises equity risk as
    shareholders become residual claimants on a lumpier stream), and the discount rate
    determines which investments pass. Note what belongs in cash flows and what doesn't:
    only <em>incremental</em> flows count (the change versus doing nothing — sunk costs are
    invisible; a cannibalised existing product line is counted), working-capital swings are
    cash flows like any other (inventory builds consume cash, even when profit looks fine),
    and inflation must be handled consistently — discount <em>nominal</em> cash flows at
    nominal rates, or strip inflation from both sides. Mixing a real rate with nominal
    flows quietly overvalues everything.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>10,000 is promised in 5 years and the discount rate is 8 per cent. Roughly what is it worth today — and why does waiting shrink it?</li>
    <li>A perpetuity pays 4,000 a year forever and is worth 100,000 at a 4 per cent rate. What happens to its value if the rate doubles to 8 per cent?</li>
    <li>A small project yields 30 per cent a year; a large one yields 12 per cent but creates more total wealth. Which number should break a tie between them, and why?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) about 6,806 — 10,000 ÷ 1.08<sup>5</sup>. Waiting shrinks value
    because money in hand could meanwhile be compounding elsewhere at the discount rate.
    (2) it halves to 4,000 ÷ 0.08 = 50,000: a perpetuity's value moves inversely with the rate,
    which is why "forever" assets are so rate-sensitive. (3) NPV, because it measures wealth
    actually created, in today's money; the small project's higher percentage is IRR talking,
    and between projects of different sizes IRR regularly crowns the poorer choice.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The time value of money is compounding run in reverse: present value divides by (1 + r) once per period of waiting.</li>
    <li>An annuity's PV is C × (1 − (1 + r)<sup>−n</sup>) ÷ r; a perpetuity collapses to C ÷ r.</li>
    <li>NPV = present value of inflows minus cost today; accept only when it is positive.</li>
    <li>The discount rate is pure time value plus a risk premium — never just inflation.</li>
    <li>Expected cash flows are probability-weighted before discounting; the spread of outcomes sets the premium.</li>
    <li>IRR can give two answers or flatter small projects; payback ignores time value and everything after the payback point.</li>
    <li>Only incremental cash flows count, and nominal flows need nominal rates.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The intermediate lessons next door put this machinery to work: <em>Stocks and Bonds</em>
    prices bonds with exactly the discounting you just practised, and <em>Diversification and
    Portfolios</em> asks where the risk premium in the discount rate should come from. Further
    on, the advanced <em>Corporate Finance</em> lesson treats NPV as the constitution of the firm.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four expert extensions. <strong>The term structure:</strong> nothing forces one discount
    rate for all horizons — bond markets quote a whole curve of rates, and long-horizon
    projects are often discounted on long-maturity rates; when the curve is steep, the
    choice of rate matters as much as the cash flows. <strong>Continuous compounding</strong>
    replaces (1 + r)<sup>n</sup> with e<sup>rt</sup> and makes the calculus clean — it is the
    native language of option pricing and fixed income. <strong>Real options:</strong> NPV
    takes future plans as fixed, but managers can <em>wait, expand, or abandon</em>; the
    value of that flexibility is exactly the option-premium insight, and it systematically
    rescues projects that naive NPV rejects (pharmaceutical R&amp;D looks NPV-negative until
    you price the option to abandon after each trial). <strong>Hyperbolic discounting:</strong>
    humans demonstrably discount the near future more steeply than the far — we choose
    110 in a month over 100 today, but 100 in a year over 110 in thirteen months — which
    contradicts the constant-rate model and explains under-saving and procrastinated
    investment. The deepest open question is society's: what rate should discount
    <em>centuries</em>? The Stern Review's climate economics used about 1.4 per cent (future
    lives count almost equally), Nordhaus argued for market-like rates near 4–5 per cent
    (respect actual behaviour and opportunity cost) — and the choice swings trillions of
    dollars of recommended climate spending. The mathematics is settled; the ethics inside
    the discount rate is not. One professional habit closes this page: because NPV is
    exquisitely sensitive to its inputs ("garbage in, gospel out"), serious practice never
    reports a single number. It reports a range — best, base and bust cases, or a Monte
    Carlo simulation over thousands of parameter draws — and asks the more useful question:
    how bad can the assumptions get before NPV turns negative? Projects whose answer is
    "very bad indeed" are robust; projects that die with one conservative assumption were
    never really positive, only precisely lucky.
  </p>
